WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.587
Now the wavelet idea.

00:00:02.037 --> 00:00:04.476
The simplest wavelet is the Haar filter.

00:00:04.926 --> 00:00:06.343
It compares neighbours.

00:00:06.793 --> 00:00:10.807
Take two returns, subtract one from the other, and scale.

00:00:11.257 --> 00:00:13.663
Big value: a sudden change.

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:15.859
Small value: smooth.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:21.586
The discrete wavelet transform, or DWT, applies this to non-overlapping pairs.

00:00:22.036 --> 00:00:24.987
One and two, three and four, and so on.

00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:27.482
Eight returns give four coefficients.

00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:29.758
That halving is called decimation.

00:00:30.208 --> 00:00:32.214
Now shift the data by one day.

00:00:32.664 --> 00:00:37.243
The pairs are now different pairs, and every DWT coefficient changes.

00:00:37.693 --> 00:00:40.217
The result depends on where you happen to start.

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:46.746
The maximal overlap transform, MODWT, uses every adjacent pair instead.

00:00:47.196 --> 00:00:49.975
One coefficient per day, no decimation.

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:53.560
Shift the data, and the coefficients simply shift with it.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.881
This is called shift invariance.

00:00:56.331 --> 00:01:00.637
For forecasting, where a new day arrives every day, that property matters.
